2. TCP SOCKET PROGRAMMING – SERVER
========================================================================

AIM To implement a TCP server using socket programming and establish
reliable communication with a client.

THEORY 1. Socket: An endpoint used by an application for network
communication. 2. TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is a
connection-oriented transport-layer protocol. 3. TCP establishes a
connection using a three-way handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, and ACK. 4. TCP
provides reliable and ordered data delivery using sequence numbers and
acknowledgements. 5. If data is lost, TCP retransmits it. It also
provides flow control and congestion control. 6. A TCP server creates a
stream socket using socket(). 7. bind() assigns the server socket a
local IP address and port number. 8. listen() makes the socket wait for
incoming connection requests. 9. accept() accepts a client connection
and returns a connected socket. 10. recv() or read() receives data,
while send() or write() sends data. 11. close() terminates the connected
socket after communication. 12. The listening socket can remain
available to accept other clients.

PROCEDURE / WORKING 1. Create a TCP stream socket. 2. Specify the server
IP address and port number. 3. Bind the socket using bind(). 4. Call
listen() to wait for client requests. 5. Use accept() to accept an
incoming client connection. 6. Receive the client’s message using recv()
or read(). 7. Send a reply using send() or write(), if required. 8.
Close the connected socket after communication. 9. Use 127.0.0.1 for
testing on the same computer, or the server’s reachable IP address for a
LAN test.

CONCLUSION A TCP server was implemented using socket programming. The
functions socket(), bind(), listen(), accept(), send(), recv(), and
close() were studied.

