3. TCP SOCKET PROGRAMMING – CLIENT
========================================================================

AIM To implement a TCP client and communicate with a TCP server using
socket programming.

THEORY 1. A socket is an endpoint that allows an application to send and
receive network data. 2. TCP is a connection-oriented transport-layer
protocol. 3. The client establishes a connection with the server using
the server’s IP address and port number. 4. The TCP three-way handshake
uses SYN, SYN-ACK, and ACK messages. 5. TCP provides reliable, ordered
delivery of a byte stream. 6. Sequence numbers and acknowledgements help
detect missing data and support retransmission. 7. socket() creates a
TCP stream socket. 8. connect() requests a connection to the server. 9.
send() or write() transmits data to the server. 10. recv() or read()
receives data from the server. 11. close() releases the socket after
communication. 12. The operating system normally assigns a temporary
local port to the client.

PROCEDURE / WORKING 1. Create a TCP stream socket. 2. Enter the server’s
IP address and port number. 3. Call connect() to request a connection.
4. Check whether the connection is successful. 5. Prepare a message and
send it using send() or write(). 6. Receive the server’s response using
recv() or read(). 7. Display the received message. 8. Close the socket
after the exchange is complete. 9. Ensure that the server is running and
that the client uses the correct IP address and port.

CONCLUSION A TCP client was implemented and communication with a TCP
server was demonstrated. The use of connect(), send(), recv(), and
close() was understood.

