4. UDP SOCKET PROGRAMMING – SERVER
========================================================================

AIM To implement a UDP server and exchange data using datagram sockets.

THEORY 1. UDP (User Datagram Protocol) is a connectionless
transport-layer protocol. 2. UDP sends independent messages called
datagrams. 3. UDP does not establish a connection or perform a handshake
before sending data. 4. UDP does not guarantee delivery, ordering, or
retransmission of lost datagrams. 5. UDP has low overhead and is
suitable for applications that need low delay. 6. socket() creates a
datagram socket using the UDP socket type. 7. bind() assigns the server
a local IP address and port number. 8. recvfrom() receives a datagram
and provides the sender’s address. 9. sendto() sends a reply to the
specified client address. 10. A single UDP server socket can receive
datagrams from multiple clients. 11. UDP commonly uses applications such
as DNS, online gaming, and real-time audio/video.

PROCEDURE / WORKING 1. Create a UDP datagram socket. 2. Prepare the
server IP address and port number. 3. Bind the socket using bind(). 4.
Wait for a client message using recvfrom(). 5. Read and process the
received datagram. 6. Use the sender address returned by recvfrom() to
prepare the reply. 7. Send the reply using sendto(). 8. Repeat the
receive and reply process as required. 9. Close the socket when the
server is stopped.

CONCLUSION A UDP server was implemented using datagram socket functions.
The use of socket(), bind(), recvfrom(), and sendto() was understood.

  ---------------------
  END OF EXPERIMENT 4
  ---------------------

