5. UDP SOCKET PROGRAMMING – CLIENT
========================================================================

AIM To implement a UDP client and exchange datagrams with a UDP server.

THEORY 1. UDP is a connectionless transport-layer protocol used to
transfer datagrams. 2. A UDP client creates a datagram socket to send
and receive messages. 3. The server is identified using its IP address
and port number. 4. sendto() sends a datagram to the specified server
address. 5. recvfrom() receives a datagram and can identify the sender.
6. UDP does not require a connection setup before data transfer. 7. UDP
does not guarantee delivery, ordering, or retransmission. 8. Each UDP
send operation transfers a separate datagram. 9. UDP has less overhead
than TCP and is useful for low-delay communication. 10. Examples of UDP
applications include DNS, online games, voice calls, and video
streaming. 11. Applications that need reliability must implement their
own acknowledgement and retransmission methods.

PROCEDURE / WORKING 1. Create a UDP datagram socket. 2. Specify the
server IP address and port number. 3. Prepare the message to be sent. 4.
Send the message using sendto(). 5. Wait for the server’s reply using
recvfrom(), if a reply is expected. 6. Display the received response. 7.
Close the socket after communication. 8. Check the server address, port,
and network connection if no response is received.

CONCLUSION A UDP client was implemented using socket programming. The
operation of sendto() and recvfrom() and the connectionless nature of
UDP were studied.

