6. DNS ANALYSIS USING DNS COMMANDS AND PACKET TRACER
========================================================================

AIM To analyse DNS application-layer packets using DNS commands and a
packet-tracer tool.

THEORY 1. DNS (Domain Name System) converts domain names into IP
addresses. 2. It allows users to access websites using names instead of
remembering IP addresses. 3. DNS uses a hierarchical structure
consisting of root servers, top-level domains, and domain name servers.
4. A DNS resolver finds the requested record using its cache or by
contacting other DNS servers. 5. An A record maps a domain name to an
IPv4 address. 6. An AAAA record maps a domain name to an IPv6 address.
7. A CNAME record specifies an alias for another domain name. 8. An MX
record identifies mail servers for a domain. 9. DNS commonly uses UDP
port 53 for queries and responses. TCP port 53 is also used in some
cases. 10. A DNS message contains a transaction ID, flags, questions,
answers, and other record sections. 11. nslookup and dig are commands
used to obtain DNS information. 12. A packet-tracer tool can display DNS
query and response packets.

PROCEDURE / WORKING 1. Connect the computer to a network and verify its
IP configuration. 2. Open the command prompt or terminal. 3. Run
nslookup followed by a domain name, for example: nslookup example.com.
4. Note the DNS server address and the IP address returned. 5. Open the
packet-tracer or packet-analysis tool. 6. Generate a DNS query by
running the command again. 7. Locate the DNS query and response packets.
8. Inspect source and destination IP addresses and UDP port 53. 9.
Observe the queried domain name, record type, transaction ID, and
answer.

CONCLUSION DNS name resolution was analysed using commands and packet
inspection. DNS records, port 53, and the structure of DNS query and
response packets were studied.

