7. PACKET ANALYSIS OF APPLICATION-LAYER PACKETS
========================================================================

AIM To capture and analyse application-layer packets using a
packet-tracer or packet-analysis tool.

THEORY 1. The application layer provides network services to user
applications. 2. HTTP and HTTPS are used for web communication. 3. DNS
is used to translate domain names into IP addresses. 4. FTP is used for
file transfer, while SMTP is used for sending email. 5. DHCP provides
automatic IP configuration to network devices. 6. Application data is
passed to the transport layer for delivery. 7. TCP or UDP adds
transport-layer information, including port numbers. 8. The IP layer
adds source and destination IP addresses. 9. A packet-analysis tool
captures and displays network traffic. 10. Packet details include source
and destination addresses, protocol, port numbers, and packet length.
11. Packet inspection helps identify protocol behaviour and
communication problems. 12. Encrypted protocols may hide application
data, although some packet metadata remains visible.

