8. IMPLEMENT SLIDING WINDOW PROTOCOLS
========================================================================

AIM To implement the Sliding Window Protocol and study efficient and
reliable frame transmission.

THEORY 1. The Sliding Window Protocol is used for flow control and error
control in data communication. 2. It allows multiple frames to be sent
before acknowledgements are received. 3. The sender window specifies the
frames that the sender is allowed to transmit. 4. The receiver window
specifies the frames that the receiver can accept. 5. Sequence numbers
identify frames and help detect duplicate or missing frames. 6. An
acknowledgement confirms successful receipt of a frame. 7. When
acknowledgements arrive, the sender window moves forward. 8. Timers help
detect missing acknowledgements and trigger retransmission. 9. Go-Back-N
retransmits the missing frame and the later outstanding frames. 10.
Selective Repeat retransmits only the specific lost or damaged frames.
11. Sliding windows improve link utilisation compared with Stop-and-Wait
transmission. 12. For k sequence bits, the sequence-number space
contains 2^k values.

PROCEDURE / WORKING 1. Select the number of sequence bits and calculate
the sequence-number range. 2. Set the sender and receiver window sizes.
3. Initialise the sender and receiver window positions. 4. Transmit
frames up to the sender window limit. 5. Check received frames and send
acknowledgements. 6. Move the sender window forward when
acknowledgements arrive. 7. If a frame is lost, use a timer to detect
the loss. 8. Retransmit frames according to Go-Back-N or Selective
Repeat rules. 9. Continue until all frames are received and
acknowledged. 10. Observe window movement, acknowledgements, and
retransmissions.

CONCLUSION The Sliding Window Protocol was implemented and studied.
Sequence numbers, window movement, acknowledgements, timers, and
retransmission methods were understood.

