9. IMPLEMENT TRAFFIC-SHAPING ALGORITHMS
========================================================================

AIM To implement traffic-shaping algorithms and study how they control
the rate of data transmission.

THEORY 1. Traffic shaping controls the rate at which packets are sent
into a network. 2. It reduces sudden bursts of traffic and helps manage
congestion. 3. Traffic shaping can reduce queue overflow and improve
traffic flow. 4. Leaky Bucket uses a finite queue to store incoming
packets. 5. Packets leave the Leaky Bucket at a fixed rate. 6. If the
bucket is full, additional packets are discarded. 7. Leaky Bucket
produces a steady output rate and does not naturally allow large bursts.
8. Token Bucket generates tokens at a fixed rate and stores them up to a
maximum capacity. 9. A packet can be transmitted when enough tokens are
available. 10. Unused tokens allow a controlled burst of traffic after
an idle period. 11. Token Bucket controls the average rate while
allowing limited bursts. 12. Leaky Bucket smooths output, whereas Token
Bucket permits controlled bursts.

PROCEDURE / WORKING 1. Leaky Bucket algorithm: 2. Set the queue capacity
and fixed output rate. 3. Read incoming packets and add them to the
queue if space is available. 4. Discard or reject packets when the queue
is full. 5. Transmit packets at the configured output rate until the
queue is empty. 6. Token Bucket algorithm: 7. Set the token-generation
rate and bucket capacity. 8. Generate tokens at the configured rate
without exceeding the capacity. 9. When a packet arrives, check whether
enough tokens are available. 10. If enough tokens exist, consume the
tokens and transmit the packet. 11. If tokens are insufficient, delay or
drop the packet according to the implementation. 12. Compare packet
delay, queue size, output rate, and dropped packets.

CONCLUSION The Leaky Bucket and Token Bucket algorithms were implemented
and studied. Their working, rate control, queue behaviour, and handling
of bursty traffic were understood.

