10. IMPLEMENT DISTANCE VECTOR ALGORITHM
========================================================================

AIM To implement the Distance Vector Routing Algorithm and determine
minimum-cost routes between network nodes.

THEORY 1. Routing selects a path for forwarding packets from source to
destination. 2. A router stores route information in a routing table. 3.
Distance Vector Routing is a dynamic routing algorithm. 4. Each router
maintains the distance and next hop for known destinations. 5. Routers
exchange routing information with their directly connected neighbours.
6. The algorithm is based on the Bellman-Ford principle. 7. The cost
through a neighbour is the link cost plus that neighbour’s advertised
cost to the destination. 8. A router selects the route with the lowest
total cost. 9. Routing tables are updated whenever a better route is
learned. 10. The repeated exchange of information continues until the
tables stop changing; this is called convergence. 11. Distance Vector
routing is simple but may converge slowly after network changes. 12.
Routing loops and the count-to-infinity problem may occur. 13. Split
horizon, route poisoning, and hold-down timers help reduce routing
problems. 14. RIP is a common distance-vector protocol and uses hop
count as its metric.

PROCEDURE / WORKING 1. Represent the network as routers connected by
links. 2. Assign a cost to each direct link. 3. Initialise each router’s
distance to itself as zero. 4. Set the costs to direct neighbours
according to link costs. 5. Set unknown destinations to infinity. 6.
Exchange distance vectors between neighbouring routers. 7. Calculate the
cost to each destination through every neighbour. 8. Update the routing
table when a lower-cost route is found. 9. Repeat the exchange and
update process until no table changes. 10. Display each router’s final
destination, minimum cost, and next hop.

CONCLUSION The Distance Vector Routing Algorithm was implemented and
studied. The Bellman-Ford principle, exchange of routing information,
routing-table updates, and convergence were understood.
